  The plugin descriptor.

  A note on punctuation before anything else, because this file has already broken the build
  once for it: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, and the IntelliJ Platform Gradle
  plugin's patchPluginXml task fails the whole build with
  "org.jdom2.input.JDOMParseException: Error on line 12: The string is not permitted within
  comments" rather than skipping the comment. The rest of this directory uses a double hyphen
  the way prose uses a dash. Do not carry that habit into this file.
  packaging/chocolatey/ash.nuspec carries the same warning for the same reason.

  NO version AND NO idea-version ELEMENT HERE, ON PURPOSE

  Both are written into this file by patchPluginXml, from `version` and
  `pluginConfiguration.ideaVersion` in build.gradle.kts. Declaring them here as well would
  mean two sources of truth for a number, and the one in this file would be the stale one,
  because patchPluginXml replaces what it finds.

  That also keeps this file out of pyproject.toml's [tool.commitizen] version_files, and
  build.gradle.kts explains at length why the plugin version does not track ASH's at all.
  Worth stating here too, because this is the file someone would reach for: commitizen
  matches its patterns per LINE and replaces the current version within every matching line,
  so a pattern aimed at a plugin version in this file would also rewrite the build number
  range in the idea-version element, which is a completely unrelated number on a completely
  unrelated schedule.

  WHY THERE IS NO depends ON A LANGUAGE PLUGIN

  The annotator is registered for every language below. ASH scans a repository and reports
  findings in whatever it finds: Python, TypeScript, Terraform, CloudFormation YAML,
  Dockerfiles, Ruby. Restricting the registration to one language would silently drop
  findings in every other file type, and a JSON or YAML finding is among the most common
  things ASH reports. com.intellij.modules.platform is the only dependency, which is what
  makes this plugin installable in every JetBrains IDE rather than only in IDEA.
-->
<idea-plugin>
    <id>io.github.awslabs.ash</id>
    <name>ASH - Automated Security Helper</name>
    <vendor url="https://github.com/awslabs">Amazon Web Services</vendor>

    <description><![CDATA[
    Runs the <code>ash</code> command line tool over the open project and shows what it
    reports as editor annotations.
    <br/><br/>
    This plugin contains no scanners and no copy of ASH. It invokes whatever <code>ash</code>
    is on your PATH, or the executable set in <b>Settings | Tools | ASH</b>, and reads the
    SARIF that ASH already writes. Install ASH separately, then run
    <code>ash dependencies install</code> to provision the scanners ASH can install itself.
    <br/><br/>
    On a host where another program answers to the name <code>ash</code>, and MSYS2 ships an
    <code>ash</code> shell on Windows, set the executable to
    <code>automated-security-helper</code>, which is ASH's unambiguous entry point.
    ]]></description>

    <depends>com.intellij.modules.platform</depends>

    <extensions defaultExtensionNs="com.intellij">
        <!-- Application level, because the path to a binary is a property of the machine. -->
        <applicationService serviceImplementation="io.github.awslabs.ash.jetbrains.AshSettings"/>

        <applicationConfigurable
                parentId="tools"
                instance="io.github.awslabs.ash.jetbrains.AshSettingsConfigurable"
                id="io.github.awslabs.ash.settings"
                displayName="ASH"/>

        <!--
          An empty language attribute means every language, including plain text and file
          types no language plugin claims. See the note above on why this is not scoped.
        -->
        <externalAnnotator
                language=""
                implementationClass="io.github.awslabs.ash.jetbrains.AshExternalAnnotator"/>
    </extensions>
</idea-plugin>
